  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nIsentropic lift in association with an incoming shortwave heads into \nUpper MI today as some patchy dense fog is already being seen in \ncentral Marquette County early this morning (i.e. Gwinn/K.I. \nSawyer). While building upper-level cloud cover will help limit fog \ndevelopment across the area, we could see patchy dense fog continue \nto grow and develop over the eastern interior U.P. before dawn this \nmorning as cloud cover remains somewhat broken still. Looking at the \nlatest medium range and CAM guidance, it does appear that most of \nthe heaviest convection will miss our area to our south today as the \nshortwave passes. That being said, there is around a 5 to 50% chance \nthat we see over a quarter inch of rainfall between now and 8 PM EDT \nover the entire Yoop, with the highest chances over the south \ncentral and west. While it's certainly not enough to put a serious \ndent in the drought conditions over the area, at this point we will \ntake anything to help with the dryness! In addition, no severe \nweather is expected as the instability looks to be too low to \ndevelop anything other than heavy rainfall at times (but again, it \ndoes look like the heavier rain showers are more likely to miss us \nto our south today). After the shortwave passes through, expect a \ngeneral lull in the precipitation late tonight, with patchy dense \nfog potentially returning across the area. \n\nAnother shortwave looks to move through the Upper Midwest on Sunday, \nbut convection chances actually appear to be a little lower over us \nthan in comparison to today's chances; the shortwave may miss us to \nour south, and even if it doesn't it still may not create enough \nforcing to get any showers or thunderstorms up. Thus, chances across \nthe U.P. tomorrow range from 10% along Lake Superior to around 30 to \n40% in the south central. As additional shortwaves phase from the \nPlains into the Canadian Prairies, the warm front of the forming low \npressure will hopefully bring more showers and non-severe \nthunderstorms to the area; as of now, the NBM shows this warm front \nhaving a 40 to 75% chance of bringing over a quarter inch of \nrainfall to the area. However, there is some concern in regards to \nsevere weather potential, as there is some modest lift lined up with \npotentially near-record PWATs (~2.00 inches), a couple thousand J/kg \nof MUCAPE, and 0-6 km bulk shear around 30 to 40 knots; therefore, \nthe severe weather threat is certainly non-zero for Monday over us \nas of this current forecast package. \n\nSome additional rain showers and thunderstorms could be seen behind \nthe warm front Monday evening into Tuesday. However, rain chances \ndiminish from the mid-week until near the end of the forecast period \nas high pressure ridging rebuilds over the central CONUS. Thus, rain \nchances will be relegated to mesoscale forcing mechanisms (for \nexample, the lake breeze) due to the lack of synoptic-scale forcing. \nBecause warm air from the Gulf is continuing to be pumped over the \nregion behind the warm front, expect the above normal temperatures \nand humid conditions seen this weekend through early next week to \ncontinue through the rest of next week.",
